cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "The Unsolved Mathematics Atlas"
message: "If you use this atlas, its formal Lean 4 core, or its meta-analyses, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Donahue"
    given-names: "Alexander S."
    alias: "0thernes"
    email: "0_0@0thernes.art"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-8168-9648"
repository-code: "https://github.com/0thernes/unsolved-mathematics"
url: "https://0thernes.github.io/unsolved-mathematics/"
abstract: >
  A reproducible, ranked, FAIR-indexed research dossier collection of 106 open
  mathematical problems across 38 fields, paired with a standalone eight-domain,
  six-kernel Lean 4.35.0 formal verification core (45 headline theorems, 10
  non-vacuity witnesses, and 14 adversarial refutations across Collatz,
  Erdős–Straus, Lonely Runner, Brocard, Primes/Twin Primes, Goldbach,
  Perfect/Mersenne, and Ramsey R(3,3)=6), automated primary-source citation and
  Crossref/DataCite retraction auditing, a deterministic BM25/dense RAG corpus,
  RO-Crate 1.1 and Croissant 1.0 metadata, a compiled AMS-LaTeX white paper, and
  documented epistemic integrity gates. Human contributions (CRediT):
  Conceptualization, Methodology, Supervision, Formal analysis
  (statement-fidelity audit), Validation, Writing – review & editing. AI tools
  were used as disclosed drafting and formalization instruments; all formal
  theorems are independently checked by six Lean 4.35.0 kernels. Not
  peer-reviewed.
keywords:
  - mathematics
  - open problems
  - conjectures
  - lean4
  - formal verification
  - meta-analysis
  - retrieval-augmented generation
  - knowledge base
license: CC-BY-4.0
version: 0.5.0
date-released: 2026-11-25
